      "id": "a9bcc6ef"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import polars as pl\n",
        "from pathlib import Path"
      ],
      "id": "0b49d570",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "ROOT = Path.cwd()"
      ],
      "id": "6394e635",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Now that you have thought about the design of the experiment, conduct analysis that is consistent with the paper. The researchers write that they use the $AES$, which you have reasoned about in an earlier question. I've given advice that instead of using this more complex concept, instead we will simply create the averages before the analysis. \n",
        "\n",
        "## State a null hypothesis\n",
        "State the sharp-null hypothesis that you will be testing.  \n",
        "\n",
        "> [Fill this in.] \n",
        "\n",
        "## Load the data \n",
        "Load the data and store it in a variable called `d`. \n",
        "\n",
        "jupyter kernelspec list"
      ],
      "id": "1aeb26ce"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "data_path = ROOT / \"data\" / \"processed\" / \"haaj_data.gzip\"\n",
        "d = pl.read_parquet(data_path)"
      ],
      "id": "1326e8ba",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Create the average views\n",
        "There are several variables that contain views about people from other areas. These are stored in the variables that start with `views_`. Create a new variable called `views_others` that is the average of these variables. \n"
      ],
      "id": "4eb773fa"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Create a plot of the views toward others that you this is informative about this data. It could be a histogram, a boxplot, or whatever form you this is most informative to a reader. It would be a bonus if you could also show the views toward individual groups, but the main point is to communicate information about `views_others`. \n"
      ],
      "id": "1f96ac3b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "What, if anything do you note about this data? \n",
        "\n",
        "> [Fill this in.]\n",
        "\n",
        "## Group by average\n",
        "Using `data.table`, group the data by `won_lottery` and report whether views toward others are generally more positive among lottery winners or lottery non-winners. This answer should be of the form `d[ , .(mean_views = ...), keyby = ...]` where you have filled in the `...` with the appropriate functions and variables. \n"
      ],
      "id": "289a6d6b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# the result should be a data.table with two columns and two rows\n",
        "hajj_group_mean = 'fill this in' \n",
        "\n",
        "# from the `hajj_group_mean` produce a single, numeric vector that is the ate. \n",
        "# check that it is numeric using `class(hajj_ate)`\n",
        "hajj_ate        =  'fill this in' "
      ],
      "id": "c86524fb",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "How much do views about other differ between those who won the lottery and those who did not? \n",
        "\n",
        "> [Fill this in.]\n",
        "\n",
        "## Randomization inference: Simulate the sharp null hypothesis\n",
        "But is this a \"meaningful\" difference? Or, could a difference of this size have arisen from an \"unlucky\" randomization? Conduct 10,000 simulated random assignments under the sharp null hypothesis to find out. (Do not just copy the code from the async, think about how to write this yourself.) \n"
      ],
      "id": "d2390e17"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## do your work to conduct the randomization inference here.\n",
        "## as a reminder, RI will randomly permute / assign the treatment variable\n",
        "## and recompute the test-statistic (i.e. the mean difference) under each permutation\n",
        "## this should be a numeric vector that has a length equal to the number \n",
        "## of RI permutations you ran\n",
        "hajj_ri_distribution = 'fill this in'"
      ],
      "id": "0ea10251",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Create a plot of `hajj_ri_distribution` that is informative about the distribution of estimated treatment effects *if the sharp null hypothesis were true*. \n",
        "\n",
        "## Randomization inference: At least as large\n",
        "How many of the simulated random assignments generate an estimated ATE that is at least as large as the actual estimate of the ATE? \n",
        "\n",
        "Conduct your work in the code chunk below, saving the results into `hajj_count_larger`, but also support your coding with a narrative description. In that narrative description (and throughout), use R's \"inline code chunks\" to write your answer consistent with each time your run your code.  \n"
      ],
      "id": "31553bdf"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# length 1 numeric vector from comparison of `hajj_ate` and `hajj_ri_distribution`\n",
        "hajj_count_larger = 'fill this in' "
      ],
      "id": "ed55a4ff",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "<!-- Inline code chunks take the following form:  -->\n",
        "<!-- The randomization inference process generated `r hajj_count_larger` estimated effects that were larger than the true effect while assuming that the sharp null was true. These inline code chunks are opened and closed with a single back tick \"`\" and the letter \"python\" followed by a space and then the name of the variable that you want to print. You can read more about them here: \n",
        "-->\n",
        "\n",
        "> [Fill this in.]\n",
        "\n",
        "## Randomization inference: one-sided p-value\n",
        "If there are `hajj_count_larger` (`python hajj_count_larger`) randomizations that are larger than `hajj_ate` (`python hajj_ate`), what is the *one-tailed* p-value? Both write the code in the following chunk, and include a narrative description of the result following your code.  \n"
      ],
      "id": "e5f2f797"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
